ear><article-title>Genetic polymorphism in the rabies virus nucleoprotein gene.</article-title><source>Virology</source><volume>209</volume><fpage>526</fpage><lpage>537</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7778285</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pntd.0000206-Cleaveland1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cleaveland</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Fevre</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name><name><surname>Kaare</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Coleman</surname><given-names>PG</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Estimating human rabies mortality in the United Republic of Tanzania from dog bite injuries.</article-title><source>Bull World Health Organ</source><volume>80</volume><fpage>304</fpage><lpage>310</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12075367</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p>The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p>The study was financed by the following supporters: The Swiss Federal Veterinary Office, the Wolfermann-N&#x000e4;geli Foundation, Emilia Guggenheim-Schnurr Foundation, the Commission for Research Partnership with Developing Countries (Jeunes Chercheurs, co-funding of the Chadian partners), and the NCCR North-South (co-funding of Jakob Zinsstag). The funding agencies had no role in study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the manuscript.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>